# Go RESTful API — Project Skeleton Rules

Use this as the default blueprint when scaffolding or extending a production-ready Go RESTful API.
Prefer this layout and conventions over flat `controller/` / `service/` / `repository/` packages.

Architecture rationale (MySQL, GORM, `net/http`, `encoding/json`, bcrypt, JWT, validator, `public_id`) lives in [`docs/architecture.md`](docs/architecture.md). Follow that document when choices conflict with older defaults.

## Project Layout (required)

```
my-api-project/
├── cmd/
│   └── api/
│       └── main.go              # Entry point: config, DB, DI wiring, router, server
├── internal/
│   ├── config/
│   │   └── config.go            # Env vars & config loading
│   ├── database/
│   │   └── mysql.go             # GORM DB init (*gorm.DB)
│   ├── <feature>/               # One package per domain (e.g. post, comment)
│   │   ├── handler.go           # HTTP transport (request/response, routing hooks)
│   │   ├── service.go           # Business logic, validation, transactions
│   │   ├── repository.go        # GORM data access
│   │   └── model.go             # Domain entities + request/response DTOs
│   ├── middleware/
│   │   └── auth.go              # Auth / JWT middleware
│   └── response/
│       └── response.go          # Unified JSON envelope & error helpers
├── migrations/
│   └── 000001_init.up.sql
├── docs/
│   └── architecture.md          # Stack & design decisions
├── .env
├── go.mod
├── go.sum
└── Makefile
```

### Layout rules

- Put **only** `main` bootstrapping in `cmd/api/main.go`. No business logic there.
- All app code lives under `internal/` (not importable by external modules).
- Organize by **feature/domain**, not by technical layer across the whole repo.
- Shared cross-cutting concerns (`config`, `database`, `middleware`, `response`) stay in their own packages — do not duplicate them inside features.
- Do **not** create top-level `controller/`, `service/`, `repository/`, `helper/`, or `app/` packages in new projects.

---

## Architecture & Data Flow

```
HTTP Request
  → middleware (auth/JWT, logging, recovery)
  → handler (decode/bind, call service, write response)
  → service (validate, resolve public_id → internal id, begin GORM tx, business rules, call repository)
  → repository (GORM via ctx + *gorm.DB / tx session)
  → domain model
  → service maps to response DTO (public_id only — never raw id)
  → handler wraps in unified JSON envelope (encoding/json)
```

Dependencies point **inward**:

- `handler` → `service` (interface)
- `service` → `repository` (interface)
- `repository` → `*gorm.DB` only
- `cmd/api` wires concrete types (constructor DI)

Never import `handler` from `service` or `repository`. Never call GORM from `handler` or put HTTP types in `repository`.

---

## Layer Responsibilities

### `cmd/api/main.go`

- Load config, open DB, build validator, construct repository → service → handler.
- Register routes on `net/http` `ServeMux` and wrap with middleware.
- Start `http.Server` with sensible timeouts.
- Panic only on fatal startup failures.

### `internal/<feature>/handler.go` (transport)

- Decode JSON body (`encoding/json`) / path / query params.
- Call service with `request.Context()`.
- Map results to the unified response envelope.
- No business rules, no SQL, no transactions.
- Path params use `public_id` (e.g. `{publicId}`), never numeric database ids.

### `internal/<feature>/service.go` (business)

- Validate input (`go-playground/validator` struct tags).
- Own transaction boundaries (`db.WithContext(ctx).Transaction(...)` or equivalent); pass the tx session into repositories.
- Resolve related records by `public_id`, validate them, then use internal `id` for FKs (see Public identifiers).
- Hash/compare passwords with bcrypt when the feature handles credentials.
- Orchestrate repository calls and domain rules.
- Map domain entities ↔ DTOs (response DTOs expose `public_id` only).
- Accept repository via **interface**, not concrete struct.

### `internal/<feature>/repository.go` (data)

- Use GORM (`*gorm.DB`) with `WithContext(ctx)` for all queries.
- Prefer GORM CRUD / association APIs; use `Raw`/`Exec` only when needed, always with bound args.
- Accept a DB/tx session so service-owned transactions work.
- Return domain models / errors — never HTTP status codes or JSON envelopes.
- Lookups by public identifier: `Where("public_id = ?", ...)`; FKs always store/use numeric `id`.

### `internal/<feature>/model.go`

- Domain entity structs for GORM (table-shaped tags), including both `ID` and `PublicID`.
- Request DTOs with `json` + `validate` tags; parent refs use `*_public_id` fields, not numeric ids.
- Response DTOs with `json` tags — include `public_id`, **omit** raw `id` (do not return GORM models that leak `id`/hashes).
- Keep feature models inside the feature package (no global `model/web` dump).

### `internal/response`

All handlers MUST return JSON via these helpers. Do not invent ad-hoc response shapes.
Encode with `encoding/json` only.

**JSON encode flags (always):** unescape Unicode, unescape `/`, preserve zero fractions (match PHP `JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE | JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES | JSON_PRESERVE_ZERO_FRACTION`).

#### Helper API (implement in `response.go`)

| Helper | Use when | HTTP status | Body |
|--------|----------|-------------|------|
| `WithData(w, data, message?)` | Single resource or non-paginated list | 200 | `{ "message"?, "data" }` |
| `WithPaginatedData(w, data, meta, links, message?)` | Paginated `/list` endpoints | 200 | `{ "message"?, "data", "meta"?, "links"? }` |
| `WithMessage(w, message, status?)` | Success/ack with no payload (default 200) | status | `{ "message" }` |
| `Error(w, message, status?)` | Failures (default 400) | status | `{ "message" }` |

```go
// Template — internal/response/response.go
package response

type Meta struct {
	CurrentPage int    `json:"current_page"`
	From        *int   `json:"from"`
	LastPage    int    `json:"last_page"`
	Path        string `json:"path"`
	PerPage     int    `json:"per_page"`
	To          *int   `json:"to"`
	Total       int64  `json:"total"`
	// Echo applied list query so clients can rehydrate UI state
	Search  string         `json:"search,omitempty"`
	Sort    string         `json:"sort,omitempty"`
	Order   string         `json:"order,omitempty"` // asc | desc
	Filters map[string]any `json:"filters,omitempty"`
}

type Links struct {
	First string  `json:"first"`
	Last  string  `json:"last"`
	Prev  *string `json:"prev"`
	Next  *string `json:"next"`
}

// WithData        → { "message"?: "...", "data": ... }
// WithPaginatedData → { "message"?: "...", "data": [...], "meta": {...}, "links": {...} }
// WithMessage     → { "message": "..." }
// Error           → { "message": "..." }
```

#### Non-paginated success

```json
{
  "message": "Post created",
  "data": { "public_id": "01HXYZ...", "title": "Hello" }
}
```

`message` is optional — omit the key when not provided. Never include numeric database `id` in `data`.

#### Message-only / error

```json
{ "message": "Post deleted" }
```

```json
{ "message": "Post not found" }
```

#### Paginated list (`GET /api/<resources>` or `/list`)

**Request query parameters (standard — support all of these):**

| Param | Example | Meaning |
|-------|---------|---------|
| `page` | `?page=2` | 1-based page index (default `1`) |
| `per_page` | `?per_page=15` | Page size (default `15`, cap e.g. `100`) |
| `search` | `?search=widget` | Free-text search across allowed columns |
| `sort` | `?sort=created_at` | Sort column (whitelist in service) |
| `order` | `?order=desc` | `asc` \| `desc` (default `asc`) |
| `filter[<field>]` | `?filter[status]=active&filter[category_public_id]=01H...` | Exact/enum filters (filter values that identify rows use `public_id`, not raw ids) |

Example request:

```http
GET /api/posts?page=2&per_page=15&search=hello&sort=created_at&order=desc&filter[status]=active
```

**Response shape:**

```json
{
  "message": "Posts retrieved",
  "data": [
    { "public_id": "01HAAA...", "title": "Widget Pro", "status": "active" },
    { "public_id": "01HBBB...", "title": "Widget Air", "status": "active" }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "current_page": 2,
    "from": 16,
    "last_page": 10,
    "path": "/api/posts",
    "per_page": 15,
    "to": 30,
    "total": 142,
    "search": "hello",
    "sort": "created_at",
    "order": "desc",
    "filters": {
      "status": "active"
    }
  },
  "links": {
    "first": "/api/posts?page=1&per_page=15&search=hello&sort=created_at&order=desc&filter[status]=active",
    "last": "/api/posts?page=10&per_page=15&search=hello&sort=created_at&order=desc&filter[status]=active",
    "prev": "/api/posts?page=1&per_page=15&search=hello&sort=created_at&order=desc&filter[status]=active",
    "next": "/api/posts?page=3&per_page=15&search=hello&sort=created_at&order=desc&filter[status]=active"
  }
}
```

**List endpoint rules:**

- Parse query params in the **handler**; pass a typed `ListQuery` (page, per_page, search, sort, order, filters) into the **service**.
- Whitelist `sort` columns and `filter` keys in the service — never pass raw client field names into SQL.
- `links.*` MUST preserve the same `search`, `sort`, `order`, `per_page`, and `filter[*]` query string; only `page` changes.
- `meta.from` / `meta.to` are `null` when `data` is empty.
- `links.prev` / `links.next` are `null` on the first / last page.
- Empty result is still `200` with `"data": []` and correct `meta.total` (usually `0`).

### `internal/config` & `internal/database`

- Read from environment (`.env` for local; never hardcode DSN in source).
- Open `*gorm.DB` with `gorm.io/driver/mysql`; configure pool via the underlying `sql.DB`.
- Default stack: **MySQL** + **GORM** (`gorm.io/gorm`, `gorm.io/driver/mysql`).
- Schema changes via `migrations/` only — do not use GORM AutoMigrate as the production migration path.

### `internal/middleware`

- Auth via JWT (`github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v5`), request ID, recovery, CORS as needed.
- Middleware wraps the mux/handler chain in `main`; features do not reimplement auth.
- Prefer user/`sub` claims that use `public_id`, not numeric database ids.

### `migrations/`

- Numbered SQL migrations (`000001_*.up.sql` / `*.down.sql`) for MySQL dialect.
- Schema changes only via migrations — never “create table” in app startup code.
- Resource tables include both `id` (PK) and unique `public_id`; FKs reference `id`.

---

## Public identifiers (`public_id`)

**Rule:** Never expose raw database `id` in API paths, request bodies, response JSON, or client-facing JWT claims. `id` remains the primary key for joins and FKs only.

### Schema

- `id` — internal PK (auto-increment / bigint). Used in foreign keys (`post_id`, `user_id`, …).
- `public_id` — unique opaque public identifier (UUID/ULID). Used in URLs and JSON.

### Resolve related records (required flow)

When a child (or any write/read) needs a parent/related row referenced by the client:

1. **Lookup** the related row by `public_id`.
2. **Validate** the record (exists; `is_active` / status; authz; soft-delete / visibility as applicable).
3. **If validation passed**, use the related row’s internal `id` for the FK / join  
   (e.g. comment stored with `post_id = 1` after resolving post `public_id`).

Do not accept or trust numeric database ids from the client. Do not use `public_id` as a foreign-key column.

### API

- Paths: `/api/posts/{publicId}`, not `/api/posts/{id}`.
- Request bodies reference parents via `post_public_id` (or equivalent), not `post_id`.
- Response DTOs: `public_id` only.

Full rationale: [`docs/architecture.md`](docs/architecture.md) §8.

---

## Coding Conventions

### Interfaces & constructors

- Define interfaces next to the consumer or in the same feature package (`Service`, `Repository`).
- Export constructors: `NewRepository()`, `NewService(repo, db, validate)`, `NewHandler(service)`.
- Wire everything in `cmd/api/main.go` (manual DI — no DI framework required).

### Context & errors

- Pass `context.Context` as the first argument on service and repository methods; use `db.WithContext(ctx)`.
- Never string-concatenate user input into GORM queries — use bound args / struct conditions.
- Prefer returning `error` from repository/service for expected failures (e.g. `gorm.ErrRecordNotFound` → not found).
- Use panic + recovery middleware only for unexpected failures, consistent with a single error strategy across the project.

### Validation

- Validate request structs in the **service** layer with `validator.Validate`.
- Tags live on request DTOs, e.g. `validate:"required,max=200,min=1"`.

### Passwords & auth

- Hash and verify passwords with `golang.org/x/crypto/bcrypt` in the service layer.
- Issue and validate tokens with `github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v5`; wire checks in `internal/middleware`.

### HTTP API style

- Prefix routes with `/api`.
- Resource-oriented paths: `/api/posts`, `/api/posts/{publicId}`.
- Methods: `GET` list/detail, `POST` create, `PUT`/`PATCH` update, `DELETE` delete.
- List endpoints accept `page`, `per_page`, `search`, `sort`, `order`, and `filter[<field>]` query params; respond with `WithPaginatedData`.
- Router: standard library **`net/http` only** (Go 1.22+ `ServeMux` patterns). Do not add chi/gin/echo/httprouter.
- JSON: **`encoding/json` only**.
- Handler signatures stay thin; route registration can live in `handler.go` (`RegisterRoutes`) or `main.go`.

### Naming

- Packages: short, lowercase, no underscores (`post`, `config`).
- Files: `handler.go`, `service.go`, `repository.go`, `model.go`.
- Exported types: `Post`, `CreateRequest`, `Response`, `Service`, `Repository`, `Handler`.
- Public identifier field: `PublicID` / JSON `public_id`.

### Makefile (expected targets)

- `make run` — run API
- `make build` — compile binary
- `make migrate-up` / `make migrate-down` — apply migrations
- `make test` — run tests

---

## Adding a New Feature (checklist)

1. Create `internal/<feature>/` with `model.go`, `repository.go`, `service.go`, `handler.go`.
2. Add migrations under `migrations/` if schema changes (`id` + `public_id`; FKs on `id`).
3. Define repository + service interfaces; implement with constructors.
4. Register routes and wire DI in `cmd/api/main.go` (`net/http`).
5. Return all payloads through `internal/response` with `public_id` only.
6. Protect private routes with `internal/middleware` (JWT) when needed.
7. For relations: resolve `public_id` → validate → use internal `id`.

---

## Do Not

- Put business logic in handlers or GORM/SQL in handlers (repositories own persistence).
- Share mutable global DB/state without constructors.
- Commit secrets, real credentials, or production `.env` values.
- Mix multiple response JSON shapes for success/error.
- Create anemic global `helper` dumping grounds — keep helpers next to the concern (`response`, `database`, or the feature).
- Import another feature’s unexported internals; extract shared code to `internal/` shared packages only when truly cross-cutting.
- Expose raw database `id` in APIs, or accept client-supplied numeric ids for lookups/FKs.
- Introduce a third-party HTTP router or JSON library without an explicit project decision.
- Store plaintext passwords or roll custom password hashing.

---

## Stack defaults (unless the user overrides)

| Concern        | Default                                                      |
|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
| Language       | Go (module mode)                                             |
| HTTP           | `net/http` (`ServeMux`)                                      |
| JSON           | `encoding/json`                                              |
| DB / ORM       | MySQL + GORM (`gorm.io/gorm`, `gorm.io/driver/mysql`)        |
| Validation     | `github.com/go-playground/validator/v10`                     |
| Passwords      | `golang.org/x/crypto/bcrypt`                                 |
| Auth           | `github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v5`                               |
| Identifiers    | `public_id` external; `id` internal PK only                  |
| Config         | Env vars via `.env` + small config loader                    |
| Migrations     | SQL files in `migrations/` (golang-migrate, MySQL)           |
| Response       | `WithData` / `WithPaginatedData` / `WithMessage` / `Error`   |
| Decisions doc  | `docs/architecture.md`                                       |

When generating code, match this skeleton and these conventions unless the user explicitly asks otherwise.
